insight:
This can be achieved by modifying the Javascript snippet provided by the user as follows:

window.location.href = "https://example.com/redirect/?name=John&email=john@gmail.com&phone=1234567";
Explanation: In this modified snippet we added three parameters (name, email, and phone) to the end of the URL in the location.href property. The question mark indicates that these are query string parameters and they will be available as variables once the page has loaded.  For example, in PHP one could access these parameters like this:

 $name = $_GET["name"]; 
 $email = $_GET["email"]; 
 $phone = $_GET["phone"]; 
In general, the syntax for accessing URL parameters varies slightly depending on the scripting language being used - e.g., for Python you may need to use something like urlparse.parse_qs(), while in Node.js or other server-side environments the parameters can typically be accessed directly via the URL object.  I hope this answers your
